  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 356 AM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nA shortwave trough axis is moving over the area early today. Water \nvapor imagery at 08Z shows an area of high atmospheric moisture \nahead of the trough axis over eastern KS, and another over the TX \npanhandle. Scattered rain showers from overnight could become more \nnumerous for the early morning hours today, before possibly tapering \noff slightly midday. Chances increase again during the afternoon and \nevening as that next batch of moisture moves in. Southerly flow is \nkeeping temperatures mild this morning in the low 60s. Warming will \nbe limited by cloud cover and rain today, so highs will likely top \nout in the upper 60s.\n\nRidging aloft will allow for drier weather Sunday and we may see a \nbit more sunshine. Highs are expected to reach the low 70s. A storm \nsystem off the PNW coast currently will progress eastward over the \nGreat Basin as a deep amplified trough tomorrow, inducing lee \ncyclogenesis in the Front Range. The surface low will eject out over \nthe High Plains early Monday in response to a lead shortwave. Strong \ndynamics with the system will allow for a 50 to 60 kt LLJ Monday \nmorning and a tight pressure gradient. Southerly winds will increase\nto near advisory criteria ahead of the main system. A more unstable \nair mass will then advect into the area as low level moisture \nincreases, but instability looks to remain fairly limited in general.\nShowers and storms could develop by midday with a more robust area \nof storms developing along a cold front during the late afternoon or \nevening. Instability should be sufficient combined with strong shear \nparameters to increase the risk for severe storms. Damaging wind and \nperhaps a brief tornado risk will exist Monday afternoon and evening.\n\nModels then differ on the evolution of the H5 and surface low. One \nsolution is for a more progressive low with the cold front moving \ninto Missouri on Tuesday, while the other possibility is for the H5 \nlow to become cutoff over KS, keeping the surface low over the area. \nAt this time, NBM keeps POP elevated through the day on Tuesday, \nwhich would be more likely if the system becomes cutoff over the \narea. Uncertainty also exists in temperatures through midweek and \nwill depend on how the system evolves. The trend should eventually be\nfor cooler conditions once high pressure is able to move into the\nregion.",
